===Previous meetings===

The match featured the same teams that competed in the [[1993 UEFA Cup final]], in which Juventus prevailed 6–1 over two legs.<ref name=background>{{cite web|title=Juventus v Dortmund background |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0253-0d802e69d1b2-0321ccb5c27e-1000--juventus-v-dortmund-background/ |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=14 January 2015 |access-date=4 November 2017 }}</ref> Their two German players in that final, [[Jürgen Kohler]] and [[Andreas Möller]], had since moved to Dortmund<ref name=twenty>{{cite web|title=20 years on: Dortmund's European champions |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e9729e32785-129364f01c3f-1000--20-years-on-dortmund-s-european-champions/ |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=28 May 2017 |access-date=4 November 2017 }}</ref> along with the Brazilian [[Júlio César (footballer, born 1963)|Júlio César]] (who did not feature in the 1997 final), while another two Dortmund players who did play in Munich – [[Stefan Reuter]] and [[Paulo Sousa]] – were also former Juventus players, and [[Matthias Sammer]] and [[Karl-Heinz Riedle]] had previously played in Italy's [[Serie A]]<ref name=twenty/> (the latter's replacement at [[SS Lazio|Lazio]] was [[Alen Bokšić]], who by 1997 had moved to Juventus).

Goalkeepers [[Angelo Peruzzi]] and his understudy [[Michelangelo Rampulla]] were the only Juventus players from 1993 in the squad for the 1997 final ([[Moreno Torricelli]] and [[Antonio Conte]] were still at the club but were not involved), with the aforementioned Kohler and Möller having switched sides. In the Dortmund squad their goalkeeper [[Stefan Klos]], striker [[Stéphane Chapuisat]]<ref name=twenty/> and midfielders [[René Tretschok]], Reuter and club captain [[Michael Zorc]] remained from four years earlier.

Besides the 1993 showpiece, the clubs had also met in the semi-finals of the [[1994–95 UEFA Cup]] with Juventus progressing to the final which they lost to [[Parma Calcio 1913|Parma]],<ref name=background/> and in the [[1995–96 UEFA Champions League group stage#Group C|group stage]] of the [[1995–96 UEFA Champions League]], with each club winning away from home, however Juventus topped the group and went on to win the trophy.<ref name=background/>

In the years to follow, Juventus and Borussia Dortmund would not meet again until [[2014–15 UEFA Champions League#Round of 16|2014–15 Champions League round of 16]]<ref name=background/> – the Italian club went through,<ref>{{cite web |title=Tévez leads Juventus to Dortmund stroll |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/021f-0e9afa448e38-eba39d3f8475-1000--tevez-leads-juventus-to-dortmund-stroll/ |website=UEFA.com |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=18 March 2015 |access-date=4 November 2017 }}</ref> meaning they won all four fixtures (1993, 1995 UEFA Cup, 1995 and 2015 Champions League) at Dortmund's [[Westfalenstadion]], with their only defeat on German soil having been in this final. Juventus reached [[2015 UEFA Champions League final|that season's final]]; coincidentally that match was again held at an [[Olympiastadion (Berlin)|Olympiastadion]] in Germany, but this time in [[Berlin]], and the outcome was another 3–1 loss, to [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]].

==Match==
===Summary===
In the 29th minute, [[Karl-Heinz Riedle]] put Dortmund ahead finishing with a low shot from inside the six yard box after [[Paul Lambert]]'s cross from the right. Five minutes later, Riedle made it 2–0 with a header from a [[Andreas Möller]] corner kick from the left. [[Christian Vieri]] later had a goal disallowed for Juventus by referee [[Sándor Puhl]] for an apparent hand-ball.<ref>{{cite news |title=Klopp, del ’97 la Juve ricorda quei tre errori dell'arbitro |url=https://www.tuttosport.com/# |access-date=17 August 2026 |work=tuttosport.com |date=24 February 2015 |language=it }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |last1=Rzouki |first1=Mina |title=Echoes of the 1997 Champions League final as Juve prepare for Dortmund |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/story/_/id/37414792/echoes-1997-champions-league-final-juventus-prepare-dortmund-clash |website=espn.com |access-date=17 August 2026 |date=23 February 2015}}</ref> 

In the second half, Juventus [[Forward (association football)|forward]] [[Alessandro Del Piero]], who had come on as a substitute, scored via a back-heel after a cross from the left by [[Alen Bokšić]] to make the score 2–1.<ref>{{cite web |title=1996/97 Borussia Dortmund 3-1 Juventus: Report |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/01e8-0e6a18185aee-6ef03a8764c4-1000--1996-97-borussia-dortmund-3-1-juventus-report/ |website=UEFA.com |access-date=17 August 2026 |date=1 August 2010 }}</ref>

In the 71st minute, 20-year-old substitute and Dortmund local boy [[Lars Ricken]] latched on to a through-pass by [[Andreas Möller]] only 16 seconds after coming onto the pitch. Ricken chipped [[Angelo Peruzzi]] in the Juve goal from over 20 yards with his first touch of the ball, to make it 3–1 for Dortmund. Ricken's goal was the fastest ever by a substitute in said event.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-juventus-wrecked-by-german-bite-1264090.html |title=Football: Juventus wrecked by German bite |work=The Independent |last1=Milne |first1=David |date=29 May 1997 |access-date=1 April 2020 }}</ref>

With [[Zinedine Zidane]] unable to make an impression for Juve against the close marking of Lambert,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/blog/2011/nov/25/joy-of-six-football-exports |last=Murray |first=Scott |title=The Joy of Six: British and Irish footballers abroad |work=[[The Guardian]] |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=25 November 2011 |access-date=28 January 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.espnstar.com/rss-feed/detail/item617249/ |title=Paul Lambert – The Norwich wizard |work=[[espnstar.com]] |access-date=4 May 2011 |url-status=usurped |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120406200748/http://www.espnstar.com/rss-feed/detail/item617249/ |archive-date=6 April 2012}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.heraldscotland.com/sport/english-football/norwich-city-manager-paul-lambert-on-his-vision-for-the-future-1.917708 |last=Gordon |first=Phil |title=Norwich City manager Paul Lambert on his vision for the future |work=[[Sunday Herald]] |date=6 September 2009 |access-date=28 January 2013}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.mirrorfootball.co.uk/opinion/columnists/michael-calvin/Ian-Holloway-Nigel-Pearson-Paul-Lambert-Kenny-Jacket-Keith-Hill-and-Eddie-Howe-the-British-Football-League-managers-capable-of-being-the-next-Roy-Hodgson-The-Michael-Calvin-Column-article411383.html |last=Calvin |first=Michael |title=Revealed: The six British Football League managers capable of being the next Roy Hodgson |work=Mirror Football |date=1 May 2010 |access-date=28 January 2013 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120609222546/http://www.mirrorfootball.co.uk/opinion/columnists/michael-calvin/Ian-Holloway-Nigel-Pearson-Paul-Lambert-Kenny-Jacket-Keith-Hill-and-Eddie-Howe-the-British-Football-League-managers-capable-of-being-the-next-Roy-Hodgson-The-Michael-Calvin-Column-article411383.html |archive-date=9 June 2012 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.scotzine.com/2011/05/paul-lambert-from-linwood-rangers-to-the-english-premier-league/ |title=PAUL LAMBERT: FROM LINWOOD RANGERS BC TO THE ENGLISH PREMIER LEAGUE |work=scotzine.com |date=3 May 2011 |access-date=28 January 2013}}</ref> the 3–1 victory gave Dortmund their first Champions League title.<ref>{{cite news |title=La GAZZETTA dello SPORT - Champions League |url=https://www.gazzetta.it/Speciali/champions_league_2007/archivio_storico/97_main.shtml |access-date=17 August 2026 |work=gazzetta.it |language=it }}</ref>

